.SH "NAME"
git-worktree-sync \- Manage a worktree project layout and sync local files across worktrees
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBclone\fP \fI<origin>\fP [\fI<dir>\fP]
\fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBadd\fP [\fB\-u\fP] [\fI<path>\fP.\|.\|.]
\fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBcommit\fP [\fB\-m\fP \fI<message>\fP]
\fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBpush\fP
\fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBpull\fP [\fB\-r\fP]
\fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBstatus\fP
.fi
.br
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Set up a project directory for development with \fBgit\-worktree\fP(1), and keep local files that the project does not track (such as \fB.env\fP or \fB.idea/\fP) in sync between its worktrees.
.sp
A project directory holds a bare clone of the project repository and any number of linked worktrees created with \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\fP \fBadd\fP.
Next to it lives a second, private repository, the \fIhub\fP, which version controls the local files.
Each worktree gets its own \fB.worktreesync\fP git directory that tracks the hub, so local files are staged, committed, pushed and pulled much like project files, using the \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP commands below.
.sp
Apart from \fBclone\fP, every command acts on the \fB.worktreesync\fP of the worktree containing the current directory, and can be run from any subdirectory of it.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.sp
\fBclone\fP \fI<origin>\fP [\fI<dir>\fP]
.RS 4
Create the project directory \fI<dir>\fP for \fI<origin>\fP (see "LAYOUT" below).
The project repository is cloned bare into \fB.remote.bare\fP, its fetch refspec is changed to \fB+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\fP so that branches are tracked as remote\-tracking branches, and it is fetched again.
The hub is created with an initial commit containing \fB.worktreeinclude\fP, and the hooks described in "HOOKS" are installed.
.sp
If \fI<dir>\fP is omitted, it is derived from \fI<origin>\fP the same way \fBgit\-clone\fP(1) does it: trailing slashes and a trailing \fB/.git\fP or \fB.git\fP are removed, and the last path component is used, so both \fBgit@host:me/foo.git\fP and \fB/src/foo/.git\fP give \fBfoo\fP.
\fI<dir>\fP may already exist only if it is empty.
.RE
.sp
\fBadd\fP [\fB\-u\fP] [\fI<path>\fP.\|.\|.]
.RS 4
Stage each \fI<path>\fP in the worktree\(cqs \fB.worktreesync\fP.
Paths may be files or directories and are relative to the current directory.
All paths are validated before anything is changed.
.sp
Each path is also recorded as an anchored entry (for example \fB/src/local.cfg\fP) in the worktree\(cqs \fB.worktreeinclude\fP and in the project repository\(cqs \fBinfo/exclude\fP, unless an entry for it is already present with or without the leading or trailing slash.
Since \fBinfo/exclude\fP lives in the bare repository, the exclusion applies to every worktree.
\fB.worktreeinclude\fP is staged when it changes.
.sp
Paths are staged with \fBgit\fP \fBadd\fP \fB\-\-force\fP, as local files are commonly ignored by the project\(cqs \fB.gitignore\fP.
For a directory this includes files ignored by a \fB.gitignore\fP inside it.
.sp
\fBadd\fP refuses paths tracked by the project repository, the worktree root, \fB.git\fP, \fB.worktreesync\fP, and paths outside the current worktree.
At least one \fI<path>\fP or \fB\-u\fP must be given.
.RE
.sp
\fBcommit\fP [\fB\-m\fP \fI<message>\fP]
.RS 4
Commit the changes staged in the worktree\(cqs \fB.worktreesync\fP.
Without \fB\-m\fP an editor is opened as for \fBgit\-commit\fP(1).
The user\(cqs git configuration applies, including commit signing.
.RE
.sp
\fBpush\fP
.RS 4
Push the worktree\(cqs \fB.worktreesync\fP commits to the hub.
If another worktree pushed first, the push is rejected; integrate its changes with \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBpull\fP \fB\-r\fP and push again.
A successful push updates the other worktrees, see "HOOKS".
.RE
.sp
\fBpull\fP [\fB\-r\fP]
.RS 4
Fetch from the hub and integrate its commits into the worktree\(cqs \fB.worktreesync\fP.
Without \fB\-r\fP, divergent histories are handled according to the \fBpull.\fP* settings in \fBgit\-config\fP(1); with \fBpull.ff=only\fP a pull after a rejected push fails, and \fB\-r\fP is needed.
.sp
As with \fBgit\-pull\fP(1), uncommitted changes to tracked files make a rebasing pull fail; commit them first.
.RE
.sp
\fBstatus\fP
.RS 4
Show the \fBgit\-status\fP(1) of the worktree\(cqs \fB.worktreesync\fP: staged and unstaged changes to synced files, and how many commits the worktree is ahead of or behind the hub.
The hub is fetched first, so the ahead/behind count is current.
Untracked files are not shown, including new files inside synced directories; use \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBadd\fP \fB\-u\fP to stage those.
.RE
.SH "OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
.RS 4
Show a usage summary and exit.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-u\fP, \fB\-\-update\fP
.RS 4
With \fBadd\fP: also stage changes to files already tracked by \fB.worktreesync\fP, including deletions, and new files inside directories listed in \fB.worktreeinclude\fP.
May be combined with \fI<path>\fP arguments.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-m\fP \fI<message>\fP, \fB\-\-message\fP=\fI<message>\fP
.RS 4
With \fBcommit\fP: use \fI<message>\fP as the commit message instead of opening an editor.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-r\fP, \fB\-\-rebase\fP
.RS 4
With \fBpull\fP: rebase local commits on top of the hub instead of following the \fBpull.\fP* configuration.
.RE
.sp
Options are rejected by commands they do not apply to.
.SH "LAYOUT"
.sp
After \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBclone\fP \fI<origin>\fP \fIproject\fP and \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\fP \fBadd\fP \fIfeat\fP, the project directory contains:
.sp
\fBproject/.remote.bare\fP
.RS 4
Bare clone of \fI<origin>\fP, shared by all worktrees.
\fBinfo/exclude\fP lists \fB.worktreesync\fP, \fB.worktreeinclude\fP and every path added with \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBadd\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBproject/.git\fP
.RS 4
A gitfile containing \fBgitdir: ./.remote.bare\fP, so that git commands such as \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\fP \fBadd\fP and \fBgit\fP \fBfetch\fP work from the project directory.
.RE
.sp
\fBproject/.worktreesync.bare\fP
.RS 4
The hub: a bare repository whose \fBmaster\fP branch holds the synced local files.
.RE
.sp
\fBproject/.worktreesync\fP
.RS 4
A regular clone of the hub, for inspecting or editing its contents from the project directory.
.RE
.sp
\fBproject/feat/.worktreesync\fP
.RS 4
The worktree\(cqs sync repository: a git directory whose work tree is the worktree itself (\fBcore.worktree=..\fP), with \fBstatus.showUntrackedFiles=no\fP to hide project files, and a \fBmaster\fP branch tracking the hub\(cqs.
It can be used directly with \fBgit\fP \fB\-\-git\-dir=.worktreesync\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBproject/feat/.worktreeinclude\fP
.RS 4
The list of synced paths, in \fBgitignore\fP(5) syntax, itself tracked by the hub.
.RE
.SH "HOOKS"
.sp
\fBclone\fP copies every file in \fBshare/git\-worktree\-sync/hooks/\fP\fINAME\fP\fB/\fP, relative to the installation prefix of the (symlink\-resolved) \fBgit\-worktree\-sync\fP script, into the hooks directory of \fB.\fP\fINAME\fP in the project directory:
.sp
\fBpost\-checkout\fP (in \fB.remote.bare\fP)
.RS 4
When \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\fP \fBadd\fP creates a worktree, set up its \fB.worktreesync\fP and check out the hub\(cqs \fBmaster\fP into it, bringing in all synced files.
Worktrees that already contain a \fB.worktreesync\fP are left alone.
.RE
.sp
\fBpost\-receive\fP (in \fB.worktreesync.bare\fP)
.RS 4
When a push moves the hub\(cqs \fBmaster\fP, fast\-forward the project directory\(cqs \fB.worktreesync\fP clone and the \fB.worktreesync\fP of every other worktree.
A worktree is skipped, and never merged, if it has unpushed commits, is not on \fBmaster\fP (for example during a rebase), or has uncommitted changes the update would overwrite.
One line per worktree is reported in the output of the push:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
remote: worktreesync: updated (project root)
remote: worktreesync: skipped b (unpushed commits, run git\-worktree\-sync pull \-r)
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Projects created before a hook was added can install it by copying it into place and making it executable.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.sp
Set up a project and share a local environment file and IDE settings with every worktree:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
$ git worktree\-sync clone git@github.com:me/foo.git
$ cd foo
$ git worktree add feat
$ cd feat
$ git worktree\-sync add .env .idea
$ git worktree\-sync commit \-m \*(Aqshare local settings\*(Aq
$ git worktree\-sync push
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
Worktrees created afterwards check out \fB.env\fP and \fB.idea/\fP automatically.
To publish later changes to them:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
$ git worktree\-sync add \-u
$ git worktree\-sync commit \-m \*(Aqupdate run configurations\*(Aq
$ git worktree\-sync push
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
If the push is rejected because another worktree pushed first, rebase onto the hub and push again.
When both changed the same file, resolve the conflict and finish the rebase with git directly:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
$ git worktree\-sync pull \-r
CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in .env
$ $EDITOR .env
$ git worktree\-sync add .env
$ git \-\-git\-dir=.worktreesync rebase \-\-continue
$ git worktree\-sync push
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SH "BUGS"
.sp
\fBadd\fP, \fBcommit\fP, \fBpush\fP, \fBpull\fP and \fBstatus\fP only work inside a linked worktree; the \fB.worktreesync\fP clone in the project directory is managed with plain git.
.sp
There is no command to continue or abort a rebase started by \fBpull\fP \fB\-r\fP; use \fBgit\fP \fB\-\-git\-dir=.worktreesync\fP \fBrebase\fP \fB\-\-continue\fP or \fB\-\-abort\fP.
.sp
Removing a synced file stages its deletion with \fBadd\fP \fB\-u\fP, but leaves its entries in \fB.worktreeinclude\fP and \fBinfo/exclude\fP.
.sp
Because untracked files are hidden in \fB.worktreesync\fP, the hints in \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fBstatus\fP output mention \fB\-u\fP; this refers to \fBgit\fP \fBstatus\fP \fB\-u\fP, not \fBgit\fP \fBworktree\-sync\fP \fB\-u\fP.
